from deep3 import d, DEEP

d("i-3", "The Septuagint, the Maccabees and the road to Herod",
 "Greek-speaking Jews, above all in Alexandria, needed their scriptures in Greek. The Letter of Aristeas tells how seventy-two elders translated the Law for Ptolemy II (285–246 BC). The story is legendary, but the Torah was probably translated in Egypt in the third century BC, and the other books over the next two centuries. This 'Septuagint' became the Bible of the early Church: most quotations of the Old Testament in the New follow it.",
 "In 167 BC the Seleucid king Antiochus IV Epiphanes, backed by a Hellenizing party in Jerusalem, banned circumcision and Sabbath observance and set up a pagan altar in the Temple. The revolt led by the priest Mattathias and his son Judas Maccabeus retook the Temple and rededicated it in 164 BC, the origin of Hanukkah. 1 and 2 Maccabees, preserved in the Greek Bible, tell the story and give early evidence of belief in resurrection and in prayer for the dead (2 Maccabees 7 and 12).",
 "The Maccabees' descendants, the Hasmoneans, ruled as high priests and later kings. Their dynasty was weakened by civil war, and in 63 BC the Roman general Pompey took Jerusalem and entered the Temple. Judea became a client state of Rome.",
 "In 40 BC the Roman Senate named Herod, an Idumean, king of Judea. He held Jerusalem from 37 BC until his death in 4 BC. He was an able, ruthless ruler and a great builder: Caesarea Maritima, Masada, the Herodium, and a vast rebuilding of the Temple begun around 20 BC. This is the world into which Jesus was born.")

d("i-5", "Roads, cities and gods: the world the first Christians travelled",
 "The Roman Empire of the first century held perhaps 50 to 60 million people around the Mediterranean. Roman roads, sea lanes cleared of pirates and a long internal peace made travel easier than at any time before the modern era. In the eastern half of the empire, common (koine) Greek was spoken in every city. The New Testament was written in it.",
 "Religion was woven into civic life. Each city honoured its gods with sacrifices and festivals, and the cult of the emperor expressed loyalty to Rome. Refusing these rites looked like disloyalty, which is why Christians, who would not take part, were called 'atheists'. Alongside civic cults, 'mystery' religions offered personal initiation and hope: the cults of Isis, of the Great Mother, of Mithras and of the Eleusinian goddesses.",
 "Philosophy offered another kind of guidance. Stoics taught living according to reason and nature; Epicureans sought freedom from fear; Platonists looked to an unchanging divine reality beyond the visible world. Christian writers from Justin onwards borrowed their vocabulary while arguing that Christ fulfilled what the philosophers had sought.",
 "Jews lived in many cities of the diaspora, with synagogues, the right to observe the Sabbath and exemption from civic sacrifice. Some Gentiles, the 'God-fearers', attended synagogues without converting. According to Acts, Paul's preaching often began in such synagogues and drew in these sympathisers. Private associations (collegia) were another familiar model: to outsiders, the house churches probably looked like one.")

d("i-6", "Rulers, priests and villages in Jesus' homeland",
 "After Herod's death in 4 BC Augustus divided the kingdom among three sons. Archelaus received Judea and Samaria but was deposed in AD 6, after which Judea was governed by Roman prefects. Herod Antipas ruled Galilee and Perea until AD 39; he is the 'Herod' of Jesus' public life. Philip ruled territories to the north-east.",
 "Pontius Pilate was prefect from 26 to 36. A dedicatory inscription found at Caesarea Maritima in 1961 names him as 'prefect of Judea'. The high priest Caiaphas held office from about 18 to 36, an unusually long tenure that suggests he cooperated closely with Rome. An ossuary inscribed 'Joseph son of Caiaphas', found in Jerusalem in 1990, may belong to his family.",
 "Galilee was a region of farming and fishing villages with two new Hellenistic-style cities built by Antipas, Sepphoris and Tiberias. The Gospels never mention Jesus visiting either. Excavations at Capernaum, Nazareth and Magdala, where a first-century synagogue was found in 2009, have filled in the picture of village life.",
 "Religious life centred on the Jerusalem Temple, its sacrifices and the great pilgrimage festivals, and on the Torah read in synagogues. Pharisees, Sadducees, Essenes and others disagreed over purity, the afterlife and how to live under foreign rule. Tensions with Rome would explode in the revolt of 66–70.")

d("ii-3", "Crucifixion in history and the earliest Easter faith",
 "Crucifixion was a Roman punishment for slaves, bandits and rebels, designed to be public and humiliating. Thousands were crucified in Judea in the first century. The heel bone of a crucified man named Yehohanan, still pierced by an iron nail, was found in a Jerusalem tomb in 1968. It confirms that victims could be nailed and could receive burial.",
 "Jesus' execution under Pilate is among the best-attested facts of ancient history. The Roman historian Tacitus (Annals 15.44, c. 116) says that 'Christus' was executed by the procurator Pontius Pilate in the reign of Tiberius. Josephus also refers to it, in a passage that was later expanded by Christian copyists.",
 "The earliest written account of resurrection belief is the formula Paul 'received' and passed on in 1 Corinthians 15:3–8, written around 54. Many scholars date the formula itself to within a few years of the crucifixion. It lists appearances to Peter, the Twelve, more than five hundred people, James and Paul. The Gospels add the empty tomb and appearances to women, whose testimony carried little legal weight at the time, a detail later writers would hardly have invented.",
 "Historians differ on what can be said. All agree that Jesus' followers sincerely believed they had seen him alive and that this belief launched the movement. Whether the resurrection happened is a question of faith and philosophy that historical method alone cannot settle; this edition presents the evidence and the range of scholarly views.")

d("ii-4", "Table fellowship, the Law and the first great dispute",
 "The first believers were Jews who kept the Law and worshipped in the Temple. The question was what to require of Gentiles who believed in Jesus: circumcision and the full Law, or something less? Acts describes Peter baptizing the Roman centurion Cornelius (Acts 10) and believers at Antioch preaching to Greeks. It was at Antioch, says Acts 11:26, that the disciples were first called 'Christians'.",
 "Two accounts of the resulting meeting in Jerusalem survive. In Acts 15 James proposes that Gentiles need not be circumcised but should abstain from food offered to idols, from blood, from strangled meat and from sexual immorality (the 'apostolic decree'). In Galatians 2 Paul says the leaders of Jerusalem, James, Cephas and John, recognized his mission and asked only that he remember the poor. Scholars debate how the two accounts relate.",
 "Soon afterwards, at Antioch, Peter stopped eating with Gentile believers when men came 'from James', and Paul opposed him 'to his face' (Galatians 2:11–14). The issue was table fellowship: whether Jewish and Gentile believers could share meals, including the Lord's Supper, without one group compromising its practice.",
 "Paul's answer, that Gentiles are justified by faith in Christ and need not become Jews, shaped Christianity decisively. His collection of money from Gentile churches for the poor in Jerusalem was meant as a sign of unity between the two wings of the movement.")

d("ii-6", "James, Peter and Paul: what the sources say about their deaths",
 "James the son of Zebedee, one of the Twelve, was beheaded by Herod Agrippa I around 44 (Acts 12:2). He is the first apostle whose death is recorded in the New Testament.",
 "James 'the brother of the Lord', leader of the Jerusalem church, was stoned in 62. The Jewish historian Josephus (Antiquities 20.200) reports that the high priest Ananus took advantage of the gap between two Roman governors to have 'James, the brother of Jesus who was called Christ' condemned. Josephus says fair-minded citizens protested and Ananus was removed. Later Christian tradition (Hegesippus) adds a more legendary account.",
 "Neither Acts nor any New Testament book narrates the deaths of Peter and Paul. The earliest evidence is 1 Clement 5 (c. 96), which speaks of their endurance and martyrdom, and Ignatius of Antioch (c. 110), who mentions both in connection with Rome. Around 200 the Roman presbyter Gaius pointed to their 'trophies' (memorials) on the Vatican hill and the road to Ostia. The tradition that both died under Nero, Peter by crucifixion and Paul by beheading, is widely accepted, though the details are later.",
 "Excavations under St Peter's Basilica in the 1940s found a modest shrine (the aedicula) built around 160 over a poor burial ground. It shows that Roman Christians venerated this spot as Peter's grave by the mid-second century. Whether his bones lie there remains disputed.")

d("ii-8", "From household gatherings to recognised ministries",
 "The first Christians met in private houses. Paul greets 'the church in their house' of Prisca and Aquila (Romans 16:5), and archaeology has found a house at Dura-Europos in Syria adapted for worship by about 240. Gatherings combined a shared meal, the Lord's Supper (1 Corinthians 11), with prayer, psalms, teaching and, in Corinth, ecstatic gifts such as speaking in tongues and prophecy (1 Corinthians 12–14).",
 "The Didache, a church manual probably from around 100, gives the earliest instructions outside the New Testament. It prescribes baptism in running water if possible, fasting on Wednesdays and Fridays, the Lord's Prayer three times a day, and short eucharistic thanksgivings over the cup and the bread. It also warns about wandering prophets who ask for money.",
 "Leadership developed unevenly. Paul writes to 'the overseers (episkopoi) and deacons' at Philippi (Philippians 1:1). Other communities were led by elders (presbyteroi). Women held recognised roles: Paul commends Phoebe, 'deacon of the church at Cenchreae' (Romans 16:1), and greets Junia as 'prominent among the apostles' (16:7). By the time of Ignatius (c. 110) a single bishop, with presbyters and deacons, led the churches he wrote to.",
 "A Roman governor gives an outside view. Around 112 Pliny the Younger reported to Trajan that Christians met before dawn on a fixed day to sing 'a hymn to Christ as to a god', and later reassembled for an ordinary, harmless meal. He tortured two slave women 'who were called deaconesses' (ministrae) to learn more.")

d("iii-4", "Why the second-century alternatives mattered",
 "Marcion, a wealthy shipowner from Sinope on the Black Sea, came to Rome and was expelled by its church around 144. He taught that the God of the Old Testament, a just but harsh creator, was a different being from the good Father revealed by Jesus. His scripture consisted of an edited Gospel of Luke and ten letters of Paul. Marcion's church spread widely and lasted for centuries.",
 "Marcion forced the other churches to say which books they accepted and why. Many historians see his list as one stimulus for defining the New Testament canon, and for insisting that the Old Testament belonged to Christian scripture.",
 "The term 'Gnosticism' covers several second-century movements that taught salvation through secret knowledge (gnosis) of a spiritual world from which souls had fallen into matter. Most of what was known about them came from their opponents until 1945, when a jar of fourth-century Coptic books was found at Nag Hammadi in Egypt. It contained the Gospel of Thomas, the Gospel of Truth and many other texts. Some scholars, such as Michael Williams and Karen King, argue that 'Gnosticism' is a modern label lumping together quite different groups.",
 "Montanism, or the New Prophecy, began in Phrygia in the 170s when Montanus and two women, Priscilla and Maximilla, prophesied in the name of the Holy Spirit and called for strict discipline. Its most famous convert was Tertullian. The movement's challenge helped the wider Church to stress the authority of bishops and the closure of revelation in the apostolic age.")

d("iii-6", "Philosophy, Scripture and the first systematic theology",
 "Tertullian of Carthage (c. 155–c. 220) was the first major Christian author in Latin. He coined or popularised much of the West's theological vocabulary: 'trinity' (trinitas), 'person' (persona) and 'substance' (substantia), especially in Against Praxeas (c. 213). He asked 'What has Athens to do with Jerusalem?' (Prescription Against Heretics 7), yet used Stoic ideas and Roman legal argument throughout his work.",
 "Clement of Alexandria (c. 150–c. 215) took a warmer view of Greek learning. In his Stromata ('Miscellanies') philosophy is a schoolmaster preparing the Greeks for Christ, as the Law prepared the Jews. His ideal is the 'true gnostic', the mature Christian whose knowledge is grounded in faith and love.",
 "Origen (c. 185–c. 253) was the greatest scholar of the early Church. His Hexapla set the Hebrew text of the Old Testament beside Greek versions in six columns. On First Principles was the first attempt at a systematic Christian theology. He read Scripture on several levels, literal, moral and spiritual, and his commentaries and homilies shaped exegesis for centuries. Driven from Alexandria, he taught at Caesarea in Palestine and died after torture in the Decian persecution.",
 "Some of Origen's speculations, such as the pre-existence of souls and the hope that all might finally be restored, were attacked in the controversies of around 400 and condemned in the sixth century. Yet Athanasius, the Cappadocians and Jerome all drew on his work.")

d("iv-1", "The Great Persecution: edicts, enforcement and survivors",
 "On 23 February 303 Diocletian's soldiers destroyed the new church at Nicomedia, and the next day the first edict was posted. It ordered churches destroyed, scriptures surrendered and burned, and Christians stripped of rank and legal privileges. Later edicts ordered the arrest of clergy, released them if they sacrificed, and in 304 required everyone to sacrifice to the gods.",
 "Enforcement varied greatly. In the West, Constantius I (Constantine's father) did little more than demolish some buildings. In the East, under Galerius and later Maximinus Daia, there were executions, mutilations and condemnations to the mines. Eusebius, an eyewitness in Palestine, recorded the Martyrs of Palestine; Lactantius, who had taught rhetoric at Nicomedia, wrote On the Deaths of the Persecutors.",
 "The persecution left lasting divisions. Clergy who had handed over scriptures were called traditores ('those who handed over'). In North Africa, disputes over whether bishops ordained by such men were valid gave rise to the Donatist schism, which divided the African church for more than a century.",
 "In April 311 the dying Galerius issued an edict of toleration: Christians might 'exist again and hold their meetings', provided they prayed for the emperor. Maximinus resumed persecution in the East until his defeat by Licinius in 313. The agreement of Constantine and Licinius at Milan that year confirmed toleration for all.")

d("iv-7", "Exiles, councils and creeds: the generation after Nicaea",
 "Athanasius became bishop of Alexandria in 328 and held the see until his death in 373, though he spent about seventeen of those years in five exiles. The Council of Tyre deposed him in 335 and Constantine sent him to Trier. He was exiled again in 339, spent 356–362 hiding among the monks of Egypt, and was briefly driven out under Julian and again under Valens.",
 "Nicaea had not ended the debate. Many eastern bishops disliked 'of one substance' (homoousios), fearing it blurred the distinction of Father and Son. Arius himself died in Constantinople in 336. The emperor Constantius II (337–361) sought a broad formula, and councils produced creed after creed.",
 "By the 350s three main positions had emerged. Homoians said only that the Son was 'like' (homoios) the Father, avoiding the language of substance; this was the emperor's preferred settlement. Homoiousians, led by Basil of Ancyra, said the Son was 'like in substance' to the Father. A radical group, the Anomoeans or Heterousians around Aetius and Eunomius, called the Son 'unlike' the Father.",
 "In 359 the twin councils of Rimini and Seleucia were pressed into accepting a homoian creed. Jerome later wrote that 'the whole world groaned and was astonished to find itself Arian'. Yet the pressure pushed homoiousians and Nicenes towards each other, preparing the settlement reached under Theodosius in 381.")

d("iv-8", "Three Cappadocians and the words for God",
 "Basil of Caesarea (c. 330–379), his younger brother Gregory of Nyssa (c. 335–395) and their friend Gregory of Nazianzus (c. 329–390) came from Christian families in Cappadocia, in central Asia Minor. Their sister Macrina, head of a household community of ascetics, shaped Basil's and Gregory's spiritual lives. Gregory of Nyssa's Life of Macrina records her influence.",
 "Their key contribution was to distinguish two words that Nicaea's anathemas had treated as synonyms: ousia (essence or substance) and hypostasis (concrete subsistence). God is one ousia in three hypostases. The Father, Son and Spirit share one divine nature and act inseparably, yet are distinguished by their relations: the Father is unbegotten, the Son begotten, the Spirit proceeding.",
 "Basil's On the Holy Spirit (375) defended giving glory to the Spirit 'with' the Father and the Son. Gregory of Nazianzus's five Theological Orations, preached in Constantinople in 380, set out the doctrine so memorably that the Eastern Church calls him 'the Theologian'. The Council of Constantinople in 381, over which he briefly presided, confirmed the Nicene faith and enlarged the creed's article on the Spirit.",
 "Against Apollinarius, who denied that Christ had a human mind, Gregory of Nazianzus wrote the principle that would guide later debates: 'What has not been assumed has not been healed' (Letter 101).")

d("v-3", "One nature, two natures: why the words still matter",
 "Cyril of Alexandria often used the phrase 'one incarnate nature (mia physis) of God the Word'. He meant that Christ is one living reality, the eternal Word who became fully human, not two subjects joined together. The phrase, which Cyril believed came from Athanasius, actually originated with Apollinarius.",
 "Eutyches, an archimandrite of Constantinople, went further, saying that after the union Christ's humanity was not 'of one substance with us'. He was condemned in 448 and again at Chalcedon in 451, whose definition confessed Christ 'in two natures, without confusion, without change, without division, without separation'.",
 "Churches that rejected Chalcedon, the Coptic, Syriac, Armenian and Ethiopian traditions among them, also rejected Eutyches. They held that 'in two natures' risked dividing Christ, as Nestorius had seemed to. Scholars now usually call them 'miaphysite' rather than 'monophysite', a term their opponents used to imply Eutychianism. The Church of the East, which stressed the two natures most strongly, is called 'dyophysite'.",
 "Part of the problem was language: physis, hypostasis and prosopon meant different things to different writers, and Greek, Syriac and Coptic terms did not map neatly onto each other. Politics mattered too: rivalry between Alexandria and Constantinople, and imperial pressure. Since the 1960s official dialogues have concluded that the Chalcedonian and non-Chalcedonian churches confess the same faith in different words.")

d("v-5", "How the old Christological divisions were revisited",
 "Unofficial consultations between Eastern Orthodox and Oriental Orthodox theologians began at Aarhus in 1964. The official joint commission's agreed statements of 1989 and 1990 (Chambésy) declared that both families had 'always loyally guarded the same authentic Orthodox Christological faith', despite using different terms. Neither side has yet formally lifted all historic anathemas.",
 "In 1973 Pope Paul VI and the Coptic Pope Shenouda III signed a common declaration confessing Christ as 'perfect God with respect to his divinity, perfect man with respect to his humanity', united 'without mingling, without commixtion, without confusion'. Similar declarations followed with the Syriac Orthodox Patriarch (1984) and the Armenian Catholicos (1996).",
 "In 1994 Pope John Paul II and Mar Dinkha IV of the Assyrian Church of the East signed a Common Christological Declaration. Each side recognized the other's expressions of faith in Christ as legitimate, while acknowledging that the Assyrians do not use the title 'Mother of God'.",
 "In 2001 Rome went further and recognized the validity of the Eucharist celebrated with the ancient Anaphora of Addai and Mari, which has no explicit words of institution. A joint international commission between the Catholic Church and the Oriental Orthodox churches has met annually since 2003.")

d("vi-3", "The Council of Ephesus (431): a council in two halves",
 "Nestorius, a monk of Antioch, became bishop of Constantinople in 428. He objected to calling Mary Theotokos ('God-bearer'), preferring Christotokos ('Christ-bearer'), because he wanted to avoid saying that God was born or suffered. Cyril of Alexandria answered with letters and twelve anathemas insisting that the Word himself was born of Mary according to the flesh.",
 "Pope Celestine condemned Nestorius at a Roman synod in 430. Emperor Theodosius II then summoned a council to Ephesus for Pentecost 431. On 22 June, Cyril opened it before John of Antioch and the Syrian bishops had arrived, and some 150 bishops deposed Nestorius the same day. Crowds in the city celebrated with torches.",
 "When John arrived a few days later, his own assembly deposed Cyril and Memnon of Ephesus. The papal legates joined Cyril's side. The emperor at first upheld both depositions and held the leaders in custody, before letting Cyril return to Alexandria. Nestorius retired to a monastery and was later exiled to Egypt, where he wrote a defence, the Bazaar of Heracleides, discovered in a Syriac version in 1895.",
 "Peace came in 433 with the Formula of Reunion, a statement drafted by the Antiochenes and accepted by Cyril, which confessed Mary as Theotokos and spoke of 'a union of two natures'. The churches of Persia, which later formed the Church of the East, never accepted Ephesus.")

d("vi-5", "Constantinople II (553): Justinian and the Three Chapters",
 "Justinian wanted to win back the miaphysite majority in Egypt and Syria. He thought that condemning three figures revered by the Antiochene tradition would show that Chalcedon was not 'Nestorian': Theodore of Mopsuestia (d. 428), the writings of Theodoret of Cyrrhus against Cyril, and a letter of Ibas of Edessa. His edict of about 544 condemning these 'Three Chapters' alarmed Western bishops, since Chalcedon had accepted Theodoret and Ibas.",
 "Pope Vigilius was brought to Constantinople in 547. He condemned the Three Chapters in 548 (the Iudicatum), withdrew under Western protest, refused to attend the council, and issued a Constitutum that spared the persons of Theodoret and Ibas. He was put under heavy pressure, and in 553–554 he finally accepted the council's decisions.",
 "The council met in May and June 553 with about 150 bishops, mostly Eastern. Its fourteen anathemas condemned the Three Chapters and interpreted Chalcedon in the light of Cyril: Christ's one hypostasis is the eternal Word, 'one of the Trinity'. Anathemas against Origenist teachings are associated with the council, but whether the council itself adopted them is debated.",
 "The miaphysites were not won back. In the West, the bishops of northern Italy broke communion with Rome over the council; this 'Three Chapters schism' in Aquileia lasted until about 700.")

d("vii-3", "New Rome: how Constantinople became a great see",
 "Constantine dedicated his new capital on 11 May 330. Its bishop at first ranked under the metropolitan of Heraclea, but the city's political weight soon raised him. Canon 3 of the Council of Constantinople (381) gave its bishop 'the prerogative of honour after the bishop of Rome, because it is New Rome'.",
 "Canon 28 of Chalcedon (451) went further. It gave Constantinople jurisdiction over the dioceses of Pontus, Asia and Thrace and 'equal privileges' with Old Rome, again because of its status as imperial city. Pope Leo I rejected the canon, arguing that the rank of sees rested on apostolic foundation, not politics. In practice it was applied in the East.",
 "From the late fifth century the bishops of Constantinople used the title 'ecumenical patriarch'. Gregory the Great protested when John IV 'the Faster' used it around 595, calling it a proud title fit for no bishop. Constantinople understood it as describing the bishop of the imperial capital (the oikoumene), not universal jurisdiction.",
 "Justinian's legislation (Novel 131, 545) ranked the five great sees: Rome, Constantinople, Alexandria, Antioch and Jerusalem, a scheme later called the pentarchy. The Council in Trullo (692) repeated canon 28.")

d("viii-6", "Photius, Pope Nicholas and the Bulgarian question",
 "In 858 Emperor Michael III's government forced Patriarch Ignatius to resign and appointed Photius, a learned layman, who was ordained through every rank in a few days. Ignatius's supporters appealed to Rome. Pope Nicholas I declared Photius deposed in 863.",
 "Meanwhile Khan Boris I of Bulgaria was baptized around 864, with Byzantine clergy. Seeking a church of his own, he turned to Rome in 866, and Latin missionaries arrived. Photius responded in 867 with an encyclical to the Eastern patriarchs attacking Latin practices in Bulgaria and, for the first time, the Western addition of the Filioque to the creed. A council in Constantinople declared Nicholas deposed.",
 "A palace coup the same year removed Photius and restored Ignatius. The Council of 869–870 condemned Photius; the West later counted it as the Eighth Ecumenical Council. But in 870 Bulgaria placed itself under Constantinople. After Ignatius died in 877 Photius was restored, and the council of 879–880, attended by papal legates, reconciled him with Rome. The East regards this second council as authoritative.",
 "In the same years Constantinople sent the brothers Constantine (Cyril) and Methodius to Moravia (863). Their Slavonic alphabet and liturgy, rejected in Moravia after Methodius's death, found a home in Bulgaria.")

d("ix-1", "Normans, azymes and reforming popes",
 "In the 1040s Norman adventurers conquered much of southern Italy, where Greek-speaking churches had long been under Constantinople. Pope Leo IX, a reformer from Alsace, led an army against them and was defeated and captured at Civitate in June 1053. Both pope and Byzantine emperor now had reason to cooperate against the Normans.",
 "Church issues pulled the other way. As the Normans and reforming popes imposed Latin customs in the south, Patriarch Michael Cerularius closed the Latin churches in Constantinople. In 1053 Archbishop Leo of Ohrid wrote a letter attacking Latin practices, above all the use of unleavened bread (azymes) in the Eucharist, which Greeks regarded as Jewish, and fasting on Saturdays.",
 "The reform papacy, from Leo IX onward, insisted on Rome's authority over the whole Church with new energy. Cardinal Humbert of Silva Candida, Leo's adviser, wrote sharp replies defending Latin customs and Roman primacy. Each side treated its own liturgical custom as the norm and the other's as error.",
 "Leo IX sent Humbert to Constantinople in 1054 to negotiate. The mission's failure produced the famous excommunications, but the conflict over bread, the Filioque and authority had been building for decades.")

d("ix-2", "Sixteenth of July 1054: what happened and what it meant",
 "The papal delegation was led by Cardinal Humbert, with Frederick of Lorraine (later Pope Stephen IX) and Archbishop Peter of Amalfi. Emperor Constantine IX received them warmly, but Patriarch Michael Cerularius refused to deal with them. Pope Leo IX died in April 1054, which arguably ended the legates' authority, but they stayed on.",
 "On Saturday 16 July 1054 the legates laid a bull of excommunication against Cerularius and his supporters on the altar of Hagia Sophia during the liturgy. The text accused them of many errors, including, ironically, removing the Filioque from the creed.",
 "On 24 July a patriarchal synod condemned the bull and anathematized the legates who had written it, not the pope or the Latin Church as a whole. Neither side excommunicated the other church. Contemporaries did not see 1054 as a final break; contacts, negotiations and even communion continued in many places.",
 "On 7 December 1965 Pope Paul VI and Patriarch Athenagoras I declared that they 'regret' and 'commit to oblivion' the excommunications of 1054. The gesture removed a symbol, but the underlying questions of authority and doctrine remain.")

d("ix-3", "Crusades, Latin patriarchs and the wound of 1204",
 "In 1095 Pope Urban II answered an appeal for help from Emperor Alexios I with a call to arms at Clermont. The First Crusade took Antioch (1098) and Jerusalem (1099). Instead of restoring Greek bishops, the crusaders installed Latin patriarchs in both cities, creating rival hierarchies on the ground.",
 "Distrust grew over the twelfth century. In 1182 a mob in Constantinople massacred many of the city's Latin residents. Westerners suspected Byzantium of hindering the crusades; Byzantines saw Latins as arrogant and dangerous.",
 "The Fourth Crusade, deep in debt to Venice, was diverted first to the Christian city of Zara (1202) and then to Constantinople to install the young Alexios IV. When he could not pay, the crusaders stormed the city in April 1204 and sacked it for three days, looting churches, relics and treasures. A Latin empire and a Latin patriarch ruled the city until 1261. For Orthodox Christians, 1204 made the schism real.",
 "Reunion attempts followed, at Lyon in 1274 and Florence in 1439, but neither was accepted by the Orthodox faithful. In 2001 Pope John Paul II expressed sorrow for the sack, and in 2004 Patriarch Bartholomew accepted the apology.")

d("x-2", "Monks, canons and friars: the shapes of medieval religious life",
 "The Rule of Benedict (c. 530) set the pattern for Western monks: stability in one community, obedience to an abbot, and a day divided between the Divine Office, work and reading. Cluny, founded in 910, built a vast network of houses under one abbot, famous for its elaborate liturgy. In reaction, the Cistercians (Cîteaux, 1098), above all under Bernard of Clairvaux, sought simplicity, manual labour and remote sites.",
 "Canons regular were priests living in community under the Rule of Augustine, serving churches and cathedrals. The Premonstratensians, founded by Norbert of Xanten in 1120, are the best-known order of canons.",
 "The friars of the thirteenth century were different again. They lived by begging (mendicancy) and moved through the growing towns to preach. Francis of Assisi's brothers received papal approval in 1209–10 and a final rule in 1223. Dominic's Order of Preachers was confirmed in 1216 to counter heresy through learned preaching. Carmelites and Augustinian friars followed. Friars dominated the new universities, where Thomas Aquinas (Dominican) and Bonaventure (Franciscan) taught.",
 "Women's religious life ranged from enclosed nuns, such as the Poor Clares founded with Clare of Assisi, to the beguines of the Low Countries, who lived in communities without permanent vows. The Fourth Lateran Council (1215) forbade founding new orders with new rules, so later groups adapted existing ones.")

d("xii-5", "Trent and the Reformation confessions, point by point",
 "Authority. Trent's fourth session (1546) received Scripture and the unwritten apostolic traditions 'with equal affection of piety', and listed a canon including the books Protestants call Apocrypha. The Reformation confessions made Scripture the sole infallible rule (sola scriptura) and generally limited the Old Testament canon to the Hebrew books, while often keeping the Apocrypha for edification.",
 "Justification. Trent's decree (1547) taught that justification is the renewal of the inner person by grace, received in baptism, with which the believer freely cooperates; faith is its beginning, and good works merit an increase of grace. The Augsburg Confession (1530, art. 4) and later confessions taught that sinners are justified freely for Christ's sake through faith alone, righteousness being imputed, with good works following as fruit.",
 "Sacraments and the Mass. Trent affirmed seven sacraments, transubstantiation (1551) and the Mass as a true propitiatory sacrifice (1562). Lutherans kept two or three sacraments and Christ's real presence without transubstantiation; Reformed confessions spoke of a spiritual presence; all rejected the Mass as a sacrifice for the living and the dead, along with purgatory and the invocation of saints.",
 "Later dialogue changed the picture on some points. In 1999 the Lutheran World Federation and the Catholic Church signed the Joint Declaration on the Doctrine of Justification, stating that the mutual condemnations of the sixteenth century do not apply to the teaching each now presents. Methodists, Reformed and Anglicans later associated themselves with it.")

d("xiv-4", "Missions, empire and the long fight against slavery",
 "Spanish and Portuguese missions travelled with conquest. Some missionaries defended native peoples: the Dominican Bartolomé de las Casas denounced abuses, and in 1550–51 debated Juan Ginés de Sepúlveda at Valladolid over whether Indians could be justly subjected. Pope Paul III's bull Sublimis Deus (1537) declared that the Indians were rational beings who must not be enslaved.",
 "The Atlantic slave trade carried some 12.5 million Africans across the ocean between the sixteenth and nineteenth centuries. Most Christian churches tolerated or defended it, and many church institutions owned slaves. Opposition grew first among Quakers: the Germantown protest of 1688, John Woolman's campaigns, and the Society of Friends' exclusion of slaveholders in the 1770s.",
 "Evangelicals such as William Wilberforce and the 'Clapham Sect' led the British campaign: the slave trade was abolished in 1807 and slavery in most British colonies in 1833. In the United States, Black churches such as the African Methodist Episcopal Church (1816) became centres of resistance; slavery ended there only with the Civil War and the Thirteenth Amendment (1865).",
 "The Protestant missionary movement, launched in earnest by William Carey's journey to India (1793), spread across Africa, Asia and the Pacific in the nineteenth century, often alongside colonial rule. Yet African and Asian Christians were increasingly the main evangelists. Samuel Ajayi Crowther, a freed slave, became the first African Anglican bishop in 1864.")

d("xv-8", "Counting Christians and the questions that divide them",
 "Estimates put the world's Christians at about 2.4 to 2.6 billion, roughly a third of humanity. The balance has shifted dramatically: in 1900 most Christians lived in Europe and North America; today most live in Africa, Latin America and Asia. Sub-Saharan Africa now has more Christians than any other region.",
 "Pentecostal and charismatic Christianity, which began with revivals such as Azusa Street in Los Angeles (1906), now counts several hundred million adherents across many denominations, with especially rapid growth in Latin America and Africa. Catholics remain the largest single body, at about 1.3 billion.",
 "In much of Europe, and increasingly in North America, church attendance and affiliation have declined sharply since the 1960s, while immigrant congregations have grown. Questions of sexuality and marriage, the ordination of women, and responses to clerical sexual abuse have divided churches internally, notably the Anglican Communion and the United Methodist Church.",
 "Christians also face persecution in several countries. Watchdog groups and governments differ on figures, but there is broad agreement that pressure on Christian minorities is severe in parts of the Middle East, Africa and Asia.")

d("p4-e", "From house meals to the great liturgies",
 "The earliest Christian worship joined a common meal with the Lord's Supper, prayer and teaching. By around 155 Justin Martyr describes a recognisable pattern for Sunday: readings from the apostles and prophets, a sermon, common prayers, the kiss of peace, the presider's thanksgiving over bread and wine mixed with water, communion, and a collection for the needy.",
 "After 313 Christians built basilicas, and worship became public and ceremonial. Regional families of liturgy took shape: the Roman, Gallican and Mozarabic in the West; the Byzantine, whose Divine Liturgies of John Chrysostom and Basil are still used; and the West Syrian, East Syrian, Coptic, Ethiopian and Armenian rites. The church year developed around Easter, with Christmas added in the fourth century.",
 "In the medieval West the Mass was in Latin, and ordinary people often received communion rarely, perhaps once a year after 1215. The Reformers introduced worship in the vernacular, congregational singing and frequent preaching: Luther's German Mass (1526), Calvin's Geneva liturgy and the Book of Common Prayer (1549). The Council of Trent responded with a standard Roman Missal (1570).",
 "The twentieth-century liturgical movement led to the Second Vatican Council's constitution Sacrosanctum Concilium (1963), which permitted the vernacular and encouraged 'full, conscious and active participation'. Many Protestant churches revised their liturgies in parallel, while Pentecostal worship, with its spontaneity and music, spread worldwide.")

d("p4-i", "Persecuted and persecutors: a sober reckoning",
 "Pre-Constantinian persecution was real but intermittent. Most historians estimate the number of martyrs in the hundreds or low thousands rather than the hundreds of thousands once claimed; the Great Persecution of 303–311 was the most severe. Its memory shaped Christian identity profoundly.",
 "Within decades of gaining power, Christians used it against others. Theodosian laws from the 380s penalised heretics and pagan sacrifice. In 415 a Christian mob in Alexandria murdered the philosopher Hypatia. Augustine, after initially opposing it, came to defend imperial coercion of the Donatists, a precedent later cited to justify force against dissent.",
 "The medieval and early modern record includes the Albigensian Crusade (1209–1229), the inquisitions (from the 1230s, and the Spanish Inquisition from 1478, whose executions modern research puts in the low thousands), the expulsions and massacres of Jews, the wars of religion culminating in the Thirty Years' War (1618–1648), and the witch trials, which killed perhaps 40,000 to 60,000 people, mostly women, between about 1450 and 1750.",
 "Christian voices for tolerance also emerged: Sebastian Castellio after the execution of Servetus in 1553, Roger Williams in Rhode Island, John Locke's Letter Concerning Toleration (1689), and the Second Vatican Council's Declaration on Religious Liberty (1965). In the twentieth century Christians were again among the most persecuted groups, under Soviet, Nazi and other regimes.")

d("p4-k", "How the texts reached us",
 "Early Christians adopted the codex, the ancestor of the book, while most literature was still copied on scrolls. Papyrus fragments such as P52, a scrap of John's Gospel usually dated to the early or mid second century, and the Chester Beatty and Bodmer papyri of around 200 are among the earliest witnesses. The great parchment codices Sinaiticus and Vaticanus date from the fourth century.",
 "About 5,800 Greek manuscripts of the New Testament survive, plus thousands in Latin, Syriac, Coptic and other languages, and countless quotations in the Fathers. They differ in hundreds of thousands of small ways, mostly spelling and word order; a few variants, such as the ending of Mark and the story of the woman taken in adultery (John 7:53–8:11), are significant and are noted in modern Bibles.",
 "Monasteries preserved much of ancient literature, Christian and classical. Cassiodorus's Vivarium in sixth-century Italy, the Irish and Northumbrian scriptoria, and the Carolingian reforms with their clear 'minuscule' script were vital links. In Byzantium the ninth-century switch to minuscule script saved many texts by recopying them.",
 "Printing changed everything. The Gutenberg Bible appeared around 1455, and Erasmus published the first printed Greek New Testament in 1516. Modern textual criticism, from Lachmann and Tischendorf, who brought Codex Sinaiticus to light between 1844 and 1859, to the Nestle–Aland editions, compares manuscripts systematically. The discoveries at Nag Hammadi (1945) and Qumran (1947) and today's digitized manuscripts continue to add to what we know.")
